The page is an isolated, heavily corrected manuscript sheet. Its neighbours are typed pages of an unrelated draft on p-adic/ultrametric analytic functions in Bourbaki style, so it has no local context. The legible content is roughly as follows.

(1) Grothendieck considers pairs (C, α): an elliptic curve C (over a finite field, or in an isogeny class determined by a Weil number ξ) together with a 'rigidification' α. After choices, all such pairs can be identified with canonical models [C(ξ), α(ξ)].

(2) He forms a group 𝒢(ξ) over the adèle ring A, described place by place:
- On A'' = A' × A_∞, with A' the finite adèles prime to p, it is the group g_{A''} 'already considered above'. On A' this is the automorphism group Γ of the prime-to-p adelic Tate module A'(1), i.e. GL_2-type data. At ∞ it is a group g_∞.
- On A_p it is a group of equivalences of a p-adic object. This is presumably the Dieudonné-module / isocrystal side, with the Frobenius T and a 'Z[1/p] on (C,T)' structure suggesting p-power quasi-isogenies.
- The note adds that this group 'is independent of ξ', and mentions a set 𝒯 and something like H^1(A, H^1).

(3) 'Hasse–Weil theory' then supplies:
- (a) an element ξ_p ∈ 𝒯_p, which he calls 'l'élément de Deuring', in 𝒢(ξ_p);
- (b) a section, unique up to some normalization, with prescribed behaviour: unit section prime to p, value at p tied to the valuation 'dominated by T', and a condition at infinity;
- giving an adelic element {ξ} whose p-component is ξ_p.

In modern terms this reads as an adelic, place-by-place description of an isogeny class of elliptic curves over F_q:
- prime-to-p level structures form a torsor under G(A_f^p);
- p-structures are governed by Dieudonné theory / Frobenius;
- the curve's own automorphism/isogeny data embed diagonally;
- Deuring's theory of reduction and lifting of CM curves identifies the Frobenius ξ_p (a Weil number / CM element) with a global element.

This is the shape later made standard as the Ihara / Langlands–Rapoport / Kottwitz description of mod-p points of modular (Shimura) curves: I(Q)\(X_p × G(A_f^p)). It also resembles the 'gerbe over adèles' with local fibre functors (ℓ-adic, crystalline, Hodge) used for motives over finite fields. Given the folder title ('Théorie de Galois des motifs'), the intended use is probably to describe a motivic Galois group(oid) of elliptic motives via CM curves and Deuring liftings.
